1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,337
What if the cloud did not live in the sky, but on the ocean floor?

2
00:00:05,337 --> 00:00:15,217
In 2018, Microsoft lowered a sealed data center module 117 feet beneath the North Sea, off Scotland's Orkney Islands.

3
00:00:16,217 --> 00:00:26,747
Inside were twelve racks, 864 standard servers, and 27.6 petabytes of storage, all surrounded by dry nitrogen.

4
00:00:27,747 --> 00:00:33,680
The capsule ran for two years without anyone opening it or replacing a single component on site.

5
00:00:34,680 --> 00:00:42,470
When engineers recovered it, the underwater servers had failed at just one-eighth the rate of a comparable land-based control group.

6
00:00:43,470 --> 00:00:52,061
Microsoft's researchers pointed to lower corrosion, stable temperatures, and the absence of people bumping hardware during repairs.

7
00:00:53,061 --> 00:01:05,031
But the experiment did not prove that every data center belongs underwater. Repairs require retrieval, marine impacts need monitoring, and the economics remain location-specific.

8
00:01:06,031 --> 00:01:16,202
It proved something narrower, and stranger: a sealed computer factory can survive on the seabed for years. The future of the cloud may be waiting below the tide.
